  "notes": "Young-band country-rock on a two-beat foundation, and the most guitar-and-harmony-forward of the 90s band acts. `bpm` spans the entry's 52–141 with `mode` at 140, inside the stated 123–141 uptempo group (\"Kick a Little\" 141, \"Life Goes On\" 140); keys are the stated E/A/D/G. Drums follow the entry sentence for sentence: \"the 140-BPM cuts run a straight 8th snare stream with a rock backbeat and quarter kick under choruses, hat closed on quarters, crash on chorus downbeats\" — `swing.grid` 8th at 0.50, `kick` [2,4] on anchors 1 and 3, `hihat.base` \"4th\", and a crash lane in `percs`. The rock reading is what separates this kit from the honky-tonk names beside it, so the accents sit at 110–127 with the fill density and open hat both higher than the Chesnutt/Tippin end of the section. Harmony is the stated I–IV–V, I–♭VII–IV and I–V–vi–IV with the ballads' vi–IV–I–V rotation. The identity device is stated plainly — \"three- and four-part band harmony on every chorus in close thirds\" — which is the countermelody weighted to octave_echo at `densityRatio` 0.6, and the arrangement keeps the entry's pre-chorus, which most of this section does not have. The bass is the other stated separator: \"more sustain than the honky-tonk names, which is what makes the band read as rock-adjacent\", hence a wider register ceiling and a raised `anticipationProb` rather than the mirror_kick lock its neighbours use. ⚠ INFERRED: velocity tiers, `percs` contents, humanize, and every melody, countermelody and bassline number. \"High tenor lead, mid-density, chorus a fifth above the verse on sustained notes\" is the source for the 57–81 register and the arch-weighted contour, and both are this author's reading. ⚠ Tempo readings [LOW-CONF] as a class; the entry states \"palette and harmony generalised from the lane [LOW-CONF]\".",
